[38;5;252m[1m## Two real bugs found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Both were in the null-pointer/faulting path, and both were invisible in [38;5;10mrelease[0m — they only appeared once I ran the suite under the [38;5;10mdev[0m profile:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. The translation borrowed the caller's raw pointers as slices via [38;5;10mslice::from_raw_parts{,_mut}[0m. That call carries a debug-assertion precondition check which [1maborts[22m on a null pointer, so Rust died with [38;5;10mSIGABRT[0m where C dies with [38;5;10mSIGSEGV[0m.[0m[0m
2. Switching to the plain [38;5;10m*p[0m deref operator did [3mnot[23m fix it — rustc emits its own null-check for raw dereferences under [38;5;10m-C debug-assertions[0m ("null pointer dereference occurred"), again [38;5;10mSIGABRT[0m instead of [38;5;10mSIGSEGV[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The fix is [38;5;10mcore::ptr::read[0m/[38;5;10mcore::ptr::write[0m, which are unchecked and match C's faulting behaviour in every profile. The nibble arithmetic itself was already bit-exact.[0m[0m
